<article class="env-theorem" id="08ZU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08ZU">Theorem <span data-tag="08ZU">5.14.4</span> <span class="named">(Tychonov)</span>.</a>  A product of quasi-compact spaces is quasi-compact. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $I$ be a set and for $i \in I$ let $X_ i$ be a quasi-compact topological space. Set $X = \prod X_ i$. Let $\mathcal{B}$ be the set of subsets of $X$ of the form $U_ i \times \prod _{i' \in I, i' \not= i} X_{i'}$ where $U_ i \subset X_ i$ is open. By construction this family is a subbasis for the topology on $X$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/08ZP" data-tag="08ZP">5.12.15</a> it suffices to show that any covering $X = \bigcup _{j \in J} B_ j$ by elements $B_ j$ of $\mathcal{B}$ has a finite refinement. We can decompose $J = \coprod J_ i$ so that if $j \in J_ i$, then $B_ j = U_ j \times \prod _{i' \not= i} X_{i'}$ with $U_ j \subset X_ i$ open. If $X_ i = \bigcup _{j \in J_ i} U_ j$, then there is a finite refinement and we conclude that $X = \bigcup _{j \in J} B_ j$ has a finite refinement. If this is not the case, then for every $i$ we can choose an point $x_ i \in X_ i$ which is not in $\bigcup _{j \in J_ i} U_ j$. But then the point $x = (x_ i)_{i \in I}$ is an element of $X$ not contained in $\bigcup _{j \in J} B_ j$, a contradiction. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>